/*
The Pure Simply Typed Lambda-Calculus
Figure 9-1, page 103

A SASyLF adaptation of some results in Types and Programming Languages
(TAPL), by Benjamin Pierce, 2002, The MIT Press.

Page numbers and references to exercises and results are from TAPL.

Fundamentals of Programming Languages
MSc in Informatics Engineering
Faculty of Sciences
University of Lisbon
2026/2027
Vasco T. Vasconcelos
*/

/*
Notes on the encoding

- Binding. A variable x is bound in a term by writing t[x] for the scope of
  the binder, as in "lambda x : T . t[x]". The substitution [x |-> t2]t12
  of TAPL is then written t12[t2]: no separate definition of substitution
  is needed.

- Values. TAPL uses a separate metavariable, v, for values. Here values are
  given by a judgment, "value t", and each v in a rule of TAPL becomes
  a premise of that form.

- Base type. The types of Figure 9-1 have no base case, so there are no
  types at all (and hence no well-typed terms; see Exercise 9.2.1).
  SASyLF rejects such a syntax as unproductive. We add an uninterpreted
  base type A, as in Figure 11-1.
*/

/*
  2.
  f x y = (f x) y
  f x y: Bool
  y tem de ser de um tipo (T2)
  f x com o tipo T2 -> Bool (aplicando y dá Bool)
  x tem de ser de um tipo (T1)
  f tem de ser do tipo T1 -> T2 -> Bool, para aplicando x dê T2 -> Bool
  Se T1 e T2 forem Bool
  Derivação:

  f:  Bool -> Bool -> Bool ∈ T            x: Bool ∈ T             
----------------------------- T-Var   ------------------ T-Var      
Γ ⊢ f : Bool -> Bool -> Bool            Γ ⊢ x : Bool                       y: Bool ∈ T
--------------------------------------------------------- T-App            -------------- T-Var
                Γ ⊢ f x : Bool -> Bool                                     Γ ⊢ y : Bool
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- T-App
                                          Γ ⊢ f x y : Bool
*/

/*
  3. 
  x x asks x to simultaneously be a function (to serve as t1) and the argument that function expects (to serve as t2). Since x can only have 
  one type at a time in any given context, this would force a function’s type to literally equal its own domain type — but a function type 
  is always “one level deeper” (one more →) than any of its components, so this can never happen. This is exactly the same self-reference
  obstruction that makes unrestricted self-application (x x, as used to build the Y-combinator or encode non-termination) impossible to 
  type in the simply typed lambda calculus — it’s precisely this restriction that gives STLC its strong normalization property, unlike 
  the untyped lambda calculus where x x (and (λx. x x)(λx. x x)) typechecks fine (vacuously, since there’s no type system) and diverges.

  É impossível.
  Por contradição, Γ ⊢ x x : T é derivável para algum Γ e T.


x: T11 -> T12 ∈ T                           x: T11 ∈ T
---------------------- T-Var          -------------------- T-Var
Γ ⊢ x : T11 -> T12                        Γ ⊢ x: T11
----------------------------------------------------------- T-App 
        Γ ⊢ x x : T (T12)

com isto T11 -> T12 = T11, nunca é satisfazivel. Se T11 é um tipo base (Bool ou Nat) então T11 -> T12 é -> -headed, mas T11 não é.
Two types with different outermost constructors can never be equal. Contradiction immediately.
If T11 = S1 → S2 for some S1, S2: then the equation T11 → T12 = T11 becomes (S1 → S2) → T12 = S1 → S2. Since → is an injective constructor 
(matching domain-to-domain, codomain-to-codomain), this forces S1 → S2 = S1 — the same shape of equation, but now with S1 playing the role 
T11 played before, and S1 is a proper (strictly smaller) subterm of T11
Logo, não é possível achar um tipo para xx.
*/

/*
  4.
  Type A — No closed term
  Regra T-Var não se aplica (x : T ∈ Γ). Contexto vazio, nada para observar
  T-App mesma coisa, apesar de serem duas sub-derivações.
  T-Abs pode ser aplicado, o problema é que o resultado sendo sempre T1 -> T2, nunca dá A.

  Type A → A - has closed term
  aplicamos T-Abs e depois T-Var a 0 ⊢ (lambda x):A.x : A -> A.

      x: A ∈ (x: A)
---------------------- T-Var
      x: A ⊢ x: A
-------------------------------- T-Abs
  0 ⊢ (lambda x):A.x : A -> A 

  (A → A) → A - No Closed Term
  No — same obstruction as A, since the only thing in scope (f : A→A) can’t manufacture an A from nothing either

  5.
  The annotation λx:T1.t isn’t just documentation — it’s what makes T-Abs’s output type a function of the term’s syntax rather than a 
  free choice. Removing it turns the rule from syntax-directed to non-deterministic (any T1 consistent with the body will do), and as soon 
  as the body doesn’t actually depend on x’s type (as in λx. true), that freedom becomes literal infinite ambiguity in what type the term 
  can be assigned — directly contradicting Uniqueness of Types.
*/

/*
1. Identifiers required for syntax and judgments
*/

terminals
    lambda // terms
    A // types
    value // judgment names


/*
2. The syntax for terms, types and contexts
*/

syntax
    t ::= x                     // variable
        | lambda x : T . t[x]   // abstraction
        | t t                   // application

    T ::= A                     // base type (not in Figure 9-1, see notes)
        | T -> T                // type of functions

    Gamma ::= *                 // empty context
            | Gamma, x : T      // term variable binding


/*
3. Judgments
*/

// Predicate value: a predicate on terms
judgment value-term: value t

---------------------------- value-abs
value (lambda x : T . t[x])


// Evaluation: a relation on pairs of terms
judgment step: t -> t

t1 -> t1'
----------------- E-App1
t1 t2 -> t1' t2

value t1
t2 -> t2'
----------------- E-App2
t1 t2 -> t1 t2'

value t2
---------------------------------------- E-AppAbs
(lambda x : T11 . t12[x]) t2 -> t12[t2]


/*
Typing: a relation on a context, a term and a type

The clause "assumes Gamma" declares Gamma as the context of the judgment:
the place where the variables that occur free in the term are bound.
In "Gamma |- t : T", term t may thus mention the variables of Gamma, and
only those.

The clause has three consequences.

- A rule may extend the context in its premises, as T-Abs does: the
  premise "Gamma, x : T1 |- t2[x] : T2" binds the variable x that the
  body t2[x] of the abstraction mentions.

- A rule for variables must take a fixed form (see T-Var below).

- SASyLF provides exchange, weakening and substitution for the judgment
  (Lemmas 9.3.6 [Permutation], 9.3.7 [Weakening] and 9.3.8 [Preservation
  of types under substitution], page 106); we need not prove them.

Judgments without the clause, such as "value t" and "t -> t'", relate
closed terms only: a variable can occur in them only under a lambda.

A theorem that mentions a context other than the empty one, *, must
repeat the clause (see theorem preservation below).
*/
judgment typing: Gamma |- t : T
assumes Gamma

/*
The form of T-Var is imposed by SASyLF: the variable is the last one in
the context. Variables further to the left are reached by weakening and
exchange.
*/
----------------------- T-Var
Gamma, x : T |- x : T

Gamma, x : T1 |- t2[x] : T2
------------------------------------------- T-Abs
Gamma |- lambda x : T1 . t2[x] : T1 -> T2

Gamma |- t1 : T11 -> T12
Gamma |- t2 : T11
-------------------------- T-App
Gamma |- t1 t2 : T12


/*
4. Progress

9.3.5 Theorem [Progress]: Suppose t is a closed, well-typed term (that is,
|- t : T for some T). Then either t is a value or else there is some t'
with t -> t'.

There is no case for rule T-Var: its conclusion, Gamma, x : T |- x : T,
needs a nonempty context, whereas p1 has the empty one, *. SASyLF sees
this from the form of p1 alone.
*/
theorem progress:
  forall p1: * |- t : T
  exists value t or t -> t'
  proof by induction on p1:
    case rule
      _: *, x : T1 |- t2[x] : T2
      ------------------------------------------- T-Abs
      _: * |- lambda x : T1 . t2[x] : T1 -> T2
    is
      _: value (lambda x : T1 . t2[x]) by rule value-abs
    end case
    case rule
      d1: * |- t1 : T11 -> T12
      d2: * |- t2 : T11
      -------------------------- T-App
      _: * |- t1 t2 : T12
    is
      ih1: value t1 or t1 -> t1' by induction hypothesis on d1
      proof by case analysis on ih1:
        case or v1: value t1 is
          ih2: value t2 or t2 -> t2' by induction hypothesis on d2
          proof by case analysis on ih2:
            case or v2: value t2 is
              // t1 is a value, hence an abstraction. No need for canonical forms
              use inversion on v1 where t1 := lambda x : T1 . t11[x]
              _: (lambda x : T1 . t11[x]) t2 -> t11[t2] by rule E-AppAbs on v2
            end case
            case or e2: t2 -> t2' is
              _: t1 t2 -> t1 t2' by rule E-App2 on v1, e2
            end case
          end case analysis
        end case
        case or e1: t1 -> t1' is
          _: t1 t2 -> t1' t2 by rule E-App1 on e1
        end case
      end case analysis
    end case
  end induction
end theorem


/*
5. Preservation

9.3.8 Lemma [Preservation of types under substitution]:
If Gamma, x:S |- t : T and Gamma |- s : S, then Gamma |- [x |-> s]t : T.

This lemma is built in. One can use it, for example, as follows:

  d5: Gamma |- t12[t2] : T by substitution on d4, d2
or
  proof by substitution on d4, d2

We state it nevertheless, writing t' and T' for s and S.
*/
lemma preservation-under-substitution:
  assumes Gamma
  forall p1: Gamma, x : T' |- t[x] : T
  forall p2: Gamma |- t' : T'
  exists Gamma |- t[t'] : T
  proof by substitution on p1, p2
end lemma

/*
9.3.9 Theorem [Preservation]:
If Gamma |- t : T and t -> t', then Gamma |- t' : T.

The proof is by induction on the evaluation derivation. There are three
cases only, one for each evaluation rule, and no nested case analysis.
In each case, inversion on p1 yields the typing derivations for the two
subterms of the application.
*/
theorem preservation:
  assumes Gamma
  forall p1: Gamma |- t : T
  forall p2: t -> t'
  exists Gamma |- t' : T
  proof by induction on p2:
    case rule
      d1: t1 -> t1'
      ----------------- E-App1
      _: t1 t2 -> t1' t2
    is
      d2: Gamma |- t1 : T11 -> T12 and
      d3: Gamma |- t2 : T11 by inversion on p1
      ih: Gamma |- t1' : T11 -> T12 by induction hypothesis on d2, d1
      proof by rule T-App on ih, d3
    end case
    case rule
      _: value t1
      d1: t2 -> t2'
      ----------------- E-App2
      _: t1 t2 -> t1 t2'
    is
      d2: Gamma |- t1 : T11 -> T12 and
      d3: Gamma |- t2 : T11 by inversion on p1
      ih: Gamma |- t2' : T11 by induction hypothesis on d3, d1
      proof by rule T-App on d2, ih
    end case
    case rule
      _: value t2
      ---------------------------------------- E-AppAbs
      _: (lambda x : T11 . t12[x]) t2 -> t12[t2]
    is
      d2: Gamma |- (lambda x : T11 . t12[x]) : T11 -> T12 and
      d3: Gamma |- t2 : T11 by inversion on p1
      d4: Gamma, x : T11 |- t12[x] : T12 by inversion on d2
      proof by substitution on d4, d3
    end case
  end induction
end theorem

/*
Exercise: Write an alternative proof of preservation, by induction on the
typing derivation, as in TAPL.
*/
theorem preservation-alternative:
  assumes Gamma
  forall p1: Gamma |- t : T
  forall p2: t -> t'
  exists Gamma |- t' : T
  proof by unproved
end theorem


/*
6. Exercises

The exercises below follow the order of the exercise sheet. Replace each
"proof by unproved" by a proof; for the exercises without a template, write
the judgments, lemmas and theorems yourself.

Tips on SASyLF (each cost us some time):

- Variables are written x, x1, x2, x', ..., as declared in the syntax; names
  such as f, y or t are not variables.

- Arrow types need parentheses, as in A -> (A -> A); so does an abstraction
  whose body is an application, as in lambda x : A . (x1 x).

- Rule T-Var types the last variable in the context only; the others are
  reached by weakening, as in

    d0: *, x1 : A |- x1 : A by rule T-Var
    d1: *, x1 : A, x2 : A |- x1 : A by weakening on d0

- Inversion on a derivation that ends with T-Var fails (SASyLF reports an
  internal error). Use case analysis instead: it has a single case.

- In a case for T-Var on a derivation of "Gamma |- t : T", the context must
  be given a new name, as in "Gamma', x : T |- x : T"; otherwise SASyLF
  reports "may not reuse context name".

- A lemma whose conclusion is contradiction cannot end a proof of another
  judgment directly. Name its result, then use it:

    c: contradiction by lemma my-lemma on d1, d2
    proof by contradiction on c
*/

// No closed value has type A
lemma no-closed-value-of-type-A:
  forall v: value t
  forall d: * |- t : A
  exists contradiction
  proof by unproved
end lemma

// Typing derivations for tru, fls and fst = λp:(A -> (A -> A)) -> A. p tru
lemma tru-typable:
  exists * |- lambda x1 : A . lambda x2 : A . x1 : A -> (A -> A)
  proof by unproved
end lemma

lemma fls-typable:
  exists * |- lambda x1 : A . lambda x2 : A . x2 : A -> (A -> A)
  proof by unproved
end lemma

lemma fst-typable:
  exists * |- lambda x : (A -> (A -> A)) -> A . (x (lambda x1 : A . lambda x2 : A . x1)) : ((A -> (A -> A)) -> A) -> A
  proof by unproved
end lemma

/*
Exercises 9.2.2 and 9.2.3, adapted to type A (see the exercise sheet).
Write x1 for f and x2, x3 for x and y. For 9.2.3, state a lemma with the
context you found.
*/
lemma derivation-1:
  exists *, x1 : A -> A, x2 : A |- x1 x2 : A
  proof by unproved
end lemma

lemma derivation-2:
  exists *, x1 : A -> A |- lambda x2 : A . (x1 (x1 x2)) : A -> A
  proof by unproved
end lemma

// Exercise 9.3.2: x x is not typable
lemma xx-not-typable:
  assumes Gamma
  forall p1: Gamma, x : T' |- x x : T
  exists contradiction
  proof by unproved
end lemma

/*
Multi-step evaluation preserves types: define the judgment t ->* t' and
prove the theorem.

The alternative proof of preservation is in section 5 above.

Uniqueness of types: define a judgment for type equality, T = T', and
prove the theorem.

Determinacy of evaluation: define a judgment for term equality, t = t',
prove that values do not evaluate, and then prove the theorem.
*/
